{
  "translated_term": "Ufundi Dijitali wa Sayansi",
  "translation_rationale": "Translating 'Digital Humanities' into Swahili requires careful consideration of both the conceptual meaning and the existing academic landscape. A direct, literal translation like 'Binadamu Dijitali' (Digital Humans) would be inaccurate and misleading. Several approaches were considered, including incorporating elements of 'Ustaarabu' (civilization/sophistication) or 'Uchimbaji' (data mining), but these proved too narrow or carried unintended connotations. \n\n'Ufundi Dijitali' (Digital Craft/Technique/Skill) captures the technological and methodological aspects inherent in Digital Humanities. It acknowledges the practical application of digital tools and techniques.  'wa Sayansi' (of Science) is appended to specify the scholarly and research-oriented nature of the field. This aligns with the academic conventions in Swahili, where compound nouns are frequently used to denote specialized disciplines. The 'wa' suffix indicates a field of study or practice related to 'sayansi' (science).  \n\nThis translation aims for scholarly accuracy by emphasizing the intersection of digital tools and humanistic inquiry. It avoids colloquialisms and prioritizes a term suitable for academic discourse. While other variations might be possible, 'Ufundi Dijitali wa Sayansi' best reflects the core meaning of 'Digital Humanities' within a Swahili-speaking academic context and is consistent with established Swahili grammatical structures for naming disciplines. It is also easily understandable to those familiar with the concept of 'sayansi' (science) as a field of study."
}
